Only 2 is an even prime, so the sieve can skip all other even numbers. The code adds 2 first, then stores and marks only odd candidates. For an odd prime `p`, its odd composite multiples begin at `p * p` and move by `2 * p`.

A quick dry run for `n = 12` gives 2 first, then keeps 3, 5, 7, and 11. Numbers such as 9 are removed when processing 3.

```go
func primeTillN(n int) []int {
	if n < 2 {
		return []int{}
	}

	isPrime := make([]bool, n+1)
	isPrime[2] = true

	for number := 3; number <= n; number += 2 {
		isPrime[number] = true
	}

	for prime := 3; prime <= n/prime; prime += 2 {
		if !isPrime[prime] {
			continue
		}

		for multiple := prime * prime; multiple <= n; multiple += 2 * prime {
			isPrime[multiple] = false
		}
	}

	primes := []int{2}
	for number := 3; number <= n; number += 2 {
		if isPrime[number] {
			primes = append(primes, number)
		}
	}

	return primes
}
```

Time complexity: O(n log log n).
Space complexity: O(n) for the marking slice.

Instead of making a full marking array, this version stores the primes already found. Each new number is tested using those primes. Testing can stop as soon as `prime * prime` is greater than the number.

This saves the sieve array, although it can do more division work than a sieve. The `long` multiplication keeps the square check safe for larger integer values.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
        public ArrayList<Integer> primeTillN(int n) {
            ArrayList<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();

            for (int number = 2; number <= n; number++) {
                boolean isPrime = true;

                for (int prime : primes) {
                    if ((long) prime * prime > number) {
                        break;
                    }
                    if (number % prime == 0) {
                        isPrime = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (isPrime) {
                    primes.add(number);
                }
            }

            return primes;
        }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n * pi(n)) as a simple upper bound, where pi(n) is the number of primes up to n.
Space complexity: O(k), where k is the number of primes stored and returned.

An input such as `n = 0` naturally leaves the list empty.

A direct version is enough here. For every number from 2 through `n`, the code checks divisors only up to its square root. If none divide it, the number is added to the answer.

For example, with `n = 10`, the checks keep `2, 3, 5, 7` and skip the composite numbers. Values below 2 return an empty list.

```python
class Solution:
    def primeTillN(self, n):
        primes = []

        for number in range(2, n + 1):
            is_prime = True
            divisor = 2

            while divisor * divisor <= number:
                if number % divisor == 0:
                    is_prime = False
                    break
                divisor += 1

            if is_prime:
                primes.append(number)

        return primes
```

Time complexity: O(n * sqrt(n)) in the worst case.
Space complexity: O(k), where k is the number of primes returned.

This keeps the work in blocks rather than creating one large sieve for the whole range. First, a small sieve finds the base primes up to `sqrt(n)`. Each block is then marked using those base primes, and its remaining values are appended.

The block size is fixed, so this version can use less working memory than a full sieve. It also handles `n < 2` without trying to build a block.

```javascript
class Solution {
    primeTillN(n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return [];
        }

        const limit = Math.floor(Math.sqrt(n));
        const baseMarked = new Uint8Array(limit + 1);
        const basePrimes = [];

        for (let number = 2; number <= limit; number++) {
            if (baseMarked[number] === 0) {
                basePrimes.push(number);

                if (number * number <= limit) {
                    for (let multiple = number * number;
                         multiple <= limit;
                         multiple += number) {
                        baseMarked[multiple] = 1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        const primes = [];
        const blockSize = 10000;

        for (let start = 2; start <= n; start += blockSize) {
            const end = Math.min(start + blockSize - 1, n);
            const marked = new Uint8Array(end - start + 1);

            for (const prime of basePrimes) {
                let first = Math.max(prime * prime,
                    Math.ceil(start / prime) * prime);

                for (let multiple = first; multiple <= end; multiple += prime) {
                    marked[multiple - start] = 1;
                }
            }

            for (let number = start; number <= end; number++) {
                if (marked[number - start] === 0) {
                    primes.push(number);
                }
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n log log n) overall, with extra block handling.
Space complexity: O(sqrt(n) + B), where B is the block size.

The result array itself is not included in the extra-space count.

The sieve marks composite numbers instead of testing each number separately. The array starts with every value marked as possible prime. When `i` is still prime, its multiples are crossed out.

Marking starts at `i * i` because smaller multiples already have a smaller factor. For example, when `n = 10`, multiples of 2 remove 4, 6, and 8, and multiples of 3 remove 9. The remaining values are collected in order.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public:
        vector<int> primeTillN(int n){
            if (n < 2) {
                return {};
            }

            vector<bool> isPrime(n + 1, true);
            isPrime[0] = false;
            isPrime[1] = false;

            for (long long i = 2; i * i <= n; ++i) {
                if (isPrime[static_cast<int>(i)]) {
                    for (long long multiple = i * i;
                         multiple <= n;
                         multiple += i) {
                        isPrime[static_cast<int>(multiple)] = false;
                    }
                }
            }

            vector<int> primes;
            for (int number = 2; number <= n; ++number) {
                if (isPrime[number]) {
                    primes.push_back(number);
                }
            }

            return primes;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n log log n).
Space complexity: O(n), not counting the returned list.

